{"analytics_identifier":null,"base_path":"/government/speeches/we-stand-with-our-partners-in-calling-for-de-escalation-and-a-return-to-diplomacy-uk-statement-at-the-un-security-council","content_id":"9c6a0f11-dbe7-41ba-a751-443c0204fc38","description":"Statement by Ambassador Kate Foster, UK Deputy Permanent Representative to the UN, at the UN Security Council meeting on Iran.","details":{"body":"<div class=\"govspeak\"><p>We meet at an important moment, following the signing of the US-Iran Memorandum of Understanding last month.</p>\n\n<p>The United Kingdom condemns the recent Iranian attacks in the region and on commercial shipping.</p>\n\n<p>We will continue to stand in solidarity with our Gulf and regional partners.</p>\n\n<p>The fragility of the current situation underscores the urgent need to keep the diplomatic process on track.</p>\n\n<p>We stand with our partners in calling for de-escalation and a return to diplomacy.</p>\n\n<p>And we welcome reports that both sides are returning to talks.</p>\n\n<p>Colleagues, the UK’s longstanding concerns over Iran’s nuclear programme remain relevant and serious.</p>\n\n<p>As a result of Iran’s significant non-performance of its JCPoA commitments, the United Kingdom, alongside France and Germany, triggered the snapback process.</p>\n\n<p>That process was completed on 28 September 2025.  </p>\n\n<p>As a result, Council resolution 2231 remains in force, and this Council should continue to remain seized of the Iranian nuclear issue.  </p>\n\n<p>The provisions of the six resolutions that were previously lifted have also come back into effect, including a binding two-way arms embargo. </p>\n\n<p>We call on all UN Member States to fully comply with the provisions of the relevant resolutions.</p>\n\n<p>The US-Iran Memorandum of Understanding underscores that Iran shall not procure or develop nuclear weapons.</p>\n\n<p>That requires clear, verifiable, and meaningful steps by Iran, including full cooperation with the IAEA and effective monitoring of all nuclear material and facilities.  </p>\n\n<p>Verification is not a technical detail.  </p>\n\n<p>It is central to trust, to confidence, and the long-term success of any agreement. </p>\n\n<p>The end result must be a negotiated settlement that strengthens international and regional security, that prevents nuclear proliferation, and provides the international community with confidence that Iran’s nuclear programme is exclusively peaceful. </p>\n\n<p>We are prepared to respond positively to credible, verifiable progress by Iran.</p>\n\n<p>And the UK stands ready to support efforts to help translate the US-Iran Memorandum of Understanding into a lasting diplomatic settlement and to address the difficult issues that remain.</p>\n</div>","change_history":[{"note":"First published.","public_timestamp":"2026-07-10T14:48:00Z"}],"delivered_on":"2026-07-10T10:00:00+01:00","first_public_at":"2026-07-10T15:48:00.000+01:00","image":{"alt_text":"","url":"https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a51064ca4890e65cce64d1b/KF_Iran_10_July.png"},"location":"United Nations, New York","political":true,"speech_type_explanation":"Transcript of the speech, exactly as it was delivered"},"document_type":"speech","first_published_at":"2026-07-10T15:48:00+01:00","links":{"available_translations":[{"api_path":"/api/content/government/speeches/we-stand-with-our-partners-in-calling-for-de-escalation-and-a-return-to-diplomacy-uk-statement-at-the-un-security-council","api_url":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/government/speeches/we-stand-with-our-partners-in-calling-for-de-escalation-and-a-return-to-diplomacy-uk-statement-at-the-un-security-council","base_path":"/government/speeches/we-stand-with-our-partners-in-calling-for-de-escalation-and-a-return-to-diplomacy-uk-statement-at-the-un-security-council","content_id":"9c6a0f11-dbe7-41ba-a751-443c0204fc38","document_type":"speech","links":{},"locale":"en","public_updated_at":"2026-07-10T14:48:00Z","schema_name":"speech","